    "interpretation": "The results mostly support using serum creatinine for adjustment. It was selected in the outcome main-effect models in all three analyses (3 of 3) and in the treatment models in two of three analyses (2 of 3), indicating that creatinine predicts the favorable outcome and is associated with treatment choice. This aligns with the clinical note that renal function can influence both treatment selection and outcome, so adjustment is reasonable for treatment models and outcome prediction. The evidence for excluding it from the treatment-effect heterogeneity model is weaker but directionally supportive: only one of three penalized interaction analyses selected the interaction, and the univariable R-learner did not improve validation loss over a constant effect (R-loss gains -0.002, 0.0, -0.001). Thus there is little evidence that treatment benefit varies meaningfully by creatinine, which is consistent with omitting it from the heterogeneity model.",
    "limitations": "The support is limited. The analyses are overlapping and from the same training dataset, so apparent consistency is not independent replication. Three analyses are few, and mixed selection (2 of 3 for treatment, 1 of 3 for interactions) means results are not robust. A positive interaction selection is not a strong positive result, and failure to achieve validation R-loss gain is not proof of no heterogeneity; it may reflect limited power, regularization, or model misspecification. Interaction evidence is partly on the log-odds scale and does not directly establish clinically important differences in the probability of favorable outcome. Effect analyses include only patients with predicted probability of treatment A between 0.1 and 0.9, so findings may not apply to patients with very high or very low predicted treatment probabilities. Residual confounding, measurement error in serum creatinine, and the binary one-year outcome definition also limit certainty."
